e from table $91BD. That value is likely the \"starting area number\" ($0760) for that level.\n\nWait, but it doesn't store to $0760 here. Hmm. It computes A, then CMP $071A... Hmm, that's odd. Then $075B=0. Hmm. This looks like it's checking something else.\n\nHmm, actually maybe this routine is the \"time up / death\" handler that decides whether to continue to the next level or restart.\n\nActually the branch at $920D sets $075B=0, then $9212 JSR $9282, $9215 JMP $9264. And $9282 checks $077A (something) and $0761, then manipulates $0753 and the lives. So $91E9 is part of the \"level clear / next level\" logic. Hmm.\n\nHmm, let me reconsider. Where is $0760 set? Let me search the ROM for \"60 07\" (operand $0760). And also find where the new game sets the level.\n\nLet me search for hex \"60 07\".\n\nAlso, I realize the task's \"area byte\" might actually be `$0760` — \"which area of the world the game loads\"! Because $9C13 uses $075F (world) and $0760 (area number) to pick the area header. And $075C might be just the displayed level digit. That fits: \"the RAM byte that selects which area of the world the game loads\" = $0760!\n\nAnd to load 2-2's underwater area, you'd set world=$075F=1 and area=$0760=some value. Hmm, but then the level number $075C matters for display.\n\nHmm, wait: $9C13 uses only $075F and $0760. So the area loaded is determined by world ($075F) and area-within-world ($0760). The level number $075C might determine the starting $0760 via some table at new game.\n\nActually hold on, maybe I have it backwards: maybe $075C = level number and $0760 = area number, and the \"area header list\" table $9CB4 per world contains offsets for each level's start area. Then $0760 is set from $075C at level start.\n\nLet me search for writes to $0760. Search hex \"8d 60 07\" (STA $0760) and \"60 07\".\n\nLet me do that.\n\nAlso let me read $0760 now. Address 1888.\n\nLet me run: read_memory 1888 len 1; search \"60 07\"; search \"8d 60 07\".\n\nLet me also s