ed to $0760 here...\n\nUnless this routine is the \"level clear → next level\" and the value A is then used by $9264. But $9264 doesn't take A; it calls $9C03 which reads $0760. Hmm, but wait — the disassembly at $9204 shows AND #$0F then CMP $071A; BEQ; BCC; LDA #0; STA $075B. So the computed value is only compared and used to set $075B (a flag). Hmm. So it's a \"does the player have the right warp?\" check, not area selection.\n\nHmm, OK. So maybe $0760 really is incremented only rarely, and the area type for each level comes from a different mechanism. Hmm.\n\nHmm, wait. Let me reconsider the header table indexing. Maybe $9CB4 is indexed by $075F but $075F is used as a \"level header base\" that changes per level... no.\n\nActually — maybe I've been wrong: perhaps $9C13's `LDY $075F` is actually reading a variable that holds the \"level number * something\". But we proved $075F is the world. Hmm.\n\nHold on, let me reconsider: maybe the SMB \"World\" number $075F is 0-7 and the \"Level\" number $075C is 0-3, and the header table $9CB4/$9CBC is indexed by world*? Let me look again: LDY $075F; LDA $9CB4,Y. With 8 worlds → 8 base entries. Then + $0760. So $0760 must be the area index. If $0760 is only incremented at the castle transition, then within a world the header doesn't change → all 4 levels share a header? That can't be.\n\nUnless $0760 is incremented each level. Let me search for other modifications of $0760, like LDA #$xx / STA $0760 (only two found) or ASL $0760, or indexed. Hmm, only 2 STA and 1 INC. Maybe DEC $0760? Let me search \"ce 60 07\".\n\nAlso, maybe the level header is not selected by $9C13 for normal play. $9C03 is called from $9264 (level start) and $8461 (next world) and $B318. Let me check: is $9C03 called at every level start? $9282/$9264 is called from $9212 (level clear) and $924C. And $9264 is reached via $9215 from $91E9. So yes, $9C03 is called at level start, using $0760.\n\nSo the header for 1-1 (0x25) came from $9CB4[0]+$0760=0 → $9CBC[0]=0x25. For 1-2, $0760 would need to be 1. But we didn't find an increment between 1-1 and 1-2. Hmm, unless the \"level clear\" path increments $0760 in a routine we haven't disassembled.\n\nWait, look at $B2FD: INC $075C; if $075C==3 then ... INC $0760. And the routine at $8461 increments $075F (world) and resets $0760=0. Hmm. So after level 3 ($075C reaches 3 = 1-4?), it checks $0748 vs $B2C2[world] and if >=, INC $0760. Hmm.\n\nHmm, $0748 might be the \"timer/hurry\" or \"score\". Hmm.\n\nI think the actual game flow might be: within a world, the 4 levels are chosen by the \"level header\" progress byte $075D? Hmm, $075D is incremented at $B312.\n\nHmm, this is a deep rabbit hole. Let me step back and take the direct empirical approach, which is what the skill recommends anyway: differential search.\n\nNew plan for the area byte: Use differential search. On the title, snapshot. Then legitimately reach a different area (or manipulate), and diff. But the area bytes don't change by playing in a level.\n\nAlternative: Since I already found that $075F (world) and $0760 (area) are read by the loader ($9C13), and $075F/$0760 together select the area header, I can PROVE $0760 by writing it and starting the game, per the task. I just need the right value for a clearly different area (underwater 2-2).\n\nLet me just empirically sweep $0760 for $075F=1. Actually, better: let me sweep for $075F=0 (World 1) with $0760=1,2,3,4 and see which gives 1-2 (underground). That's a single-byte write (only $0760 changes) which exactly matches \"the RAM byte that selects which area of the world the game loads\"! And I can prove it with 1-2 underground.\n\nBut wait, does $0760 alone control it? The loader index = $9CB4[$075F] + $0760. With $075F=0 (World 1), $0760=1 → $9CBC[1]=0x29. If 0x29 is 1-2 (underground), great. Let me test that first — it's the cleanest single-byte proof.\n\nTest: load_state 2 (title, world0/level0/area0), write $0760=1 (address 1888), press Start, screensh