o area type; there are 4 palette blocks. Overworld=1 (selector 2 = $8CC8). Underwater might be 0 (selector 1 = $8CA4) or 2 or 3.\n\nLet me just sweep. For world 1 ($075F=1), the entries are $9CBC[5..9] = [0x28,0x29,0xC0,0x26,0x60]. The one with $074E=2 is 0x60 (index9, so $0760=4). 0xC0 also $074E=2 but that's castle (bits 6-7). Let me test $0760=4 with $075F=1 → maybe underwater 2-2!\n\nActually, hmm, wait. Let me reconsider the world base. $9CB4[1]=5, so World 2 = indices 5-9 = 2-1,2-2,2-3,2-4,2-? So 2-2 = index6 = 0x29 → same header as 1-2 (index1=0x29). Hmm, so 1-2 and 2-2 share header 0x29?! That can't both be right (one underground, one underwater). Unless the difference is in the layout data, not the header. The header's low bits index the layout table; different worlds' areas at the same index could differ... no, the header index is global.\n\nHmm, actually $9D2C/$9D4E are global tables; the header 0x29 with $074E=1, $074F=9 indexes $9D28[1]+9. Same for 1-2 and 2-2 → same layout pointer! So 1-2 and 2-2 would be the same layout. That's definitely wrong.\n\nSo my assumption that $9CBC is indexed by base[world]+$0760 must be wrong, OR the world base table isn't per-world.\n\nHmm, let me reconsider $9CB4 = [0,5,10,14,19,23,27,32]. Maybe it's indexed by something else (e.g., area type or level header). And $075F is added? No, LDY $075F; LDA $9CB4,Y.\n\nHmm, so base[$075F]. For $075F=1 → 5. Our loaded header for $0760=1 was $9CBC[6]=0x29. For 1-2 ($075F=0,$0760=1) → $9CBC[1]=0x29. Same. So yes, 2-2 would share with 1-2. Unless the game never sets $0760=1 for level 2 (maybe $0760 is an area counter that's incremented to skip sub-areas).\n\nI think the real structure: $0760 is a *global area counter* that runs through the entire game's area list in a specific order, and $9CB4[world] is the starting index for that world. The areas within a world include sub-areas (like 1-2's coin rooms) so the count is 4-5 per world. The header value at each index encodes the area type and layout. 1-2 and 2-2 would be at different indices if the counts differ. Let me recompute: world 0's areas are indices 0-4 (5 areas): 0=1-1, 1=1-2, 2=1-2 bonus?, 3=1-3, 4=1-4? Then world 1's start index 5 = 2-1, 6=2-2, 7=2-2 bonus?, 8=2-3, 9=2-4. Hmm, base[2]=10 then world 2 = 3-1. Diff world0→1 = 5, world1→2 = 5, world2→3 = 4. Hmm, worlds have different counts.\n\nBut then 2-2 = index 6 = 0x29 = same header as 1-2 = index 1 = 0x29. Still same. Hmm, unless world 0's indices are 0=1-1,1=1-2a,2=1-2b,3=1-3,4=1-4 and then 1-2 header is at index1=0x29, and 2-2's index would be 5+? Let me recount: if world 0 has 5 entries (0-4) then world 1 starts at 5. 2-1=5, 2-2=6. Both 1-2 and 2-2 at 0x29. Still identical.\n\nSo the header value 0x29 is shared. That means 1-2 and 2-2 have the same header but presumably different layout via... no, the loader uses the header to index a global layout table.\n\nHmm, unless the loader's index also includes $075F somehow. Let me re-read $9C22: LDY $074E; LDA $9D28,Y; CLC; ADC $074F; TAY; LDA $9D2C,Y → $E7. So layout index = $9D28[$074E] + $074F. $074E and $074F come only from the header byte $0750. So same header → same layout. So 1-2 and 2-2 identical. That's wrong.\n\nTherefore, my reading of $9C13 must be incomplete: maybe there's an additional offset using $075F or $075C. Let me re-read $9C13:\n```\n$9C13: AC 5F 07    LDY $075F\n$9C16: B9 B4 9C    LDA $9CB4,Y\n$9C19: 18          CLC\n$9C1A: 6D 60 07    ADC $0760\n$9C1D: A8          TAY\n$9C1E: B9 BC 9C    LDA $9CBC,Y\n$9C21: 60          RTS\n```\nYes. So header = $9CBC[ $9CB4[$075F] + $0760 ].\n\nHmm. So if 1-2 and 2-2 both map to 0x29, they'd be identical levels, which is false. Unless the game never uses $0760=1 for both. I.e., the game's actual area sequence for world 1 might be 0,1,2,3,4 with 1-2 at index 1 and 1-3 at index 2... and world 2's sequence 5,6,7,8,9 with 2-1=5, 2-2=6. So both 1-2 and 2-2 = index ... no, 1 is 1-2 and 6 is 2-2. 0x29 both. So they'd be identical. Contradiction unless SMB reuses area layouts (possible! but 1-2 underground vs 2-2 underwater can't be the same).\n\nTherefore my understanding of $9CB4/$9CBC is wrong, OR the world/level→$0760 mapping means 2-2 uses a different $0760 than 6-base. Hmm.\n\nOK, let me just empirically find the underwater area. I'll sweep $075F=1, $0760=0..9 and look for an underwater (blue/teal, coral) level. We did $0760=0 (fortress-ish with big brick tower) and $0760=1 (fortress+pipe). Let me try 2,3,4 then maybe 5-9.\n\nHmm, but this is slow. Let me think about which header gives underwater. Actually, let me look at the palette blocks to see which is the underwater palette. Let me read PPU palette RAM in a known underwater level... but we have none.\n\nAlternatively, read the palette blocks at prgRom $8CA4/$8CC8/$8CEC/$8D10. The underwater palette should have a blue backdrop (e.g., $0C or $1C or $22?) and blue-ish colors. Let me read those 4 blocks. The overworld block $8CC8 has backdrop $22 periwinkle. The underwater one might have backdrop $0F black or blue.\n\nLet me read prgRom offsets 0xCA4 (3236) length 40, and 0xCC8 (3272), 0xCEC (3308), 0xD10 (3344).\n\nActually, the palette record format is [3F 00 20 <32 bytes> 00]. So at 0xCA4: 3F 00 20 then 32 palette bytes. Let me read 40 bytes from 3236.\n\nLet me read all four.\n\nThen I can identify which $074E is underwater by its palette.\n\nLet me do that."}
