{"type":"thinking","text":"Both mappings recorded and promoted. Now let me write the final report.\n\nLet me summarize the two addresses, encodings, and proofs.\n\n1. Area byte: $0760 (CPU RAM, 1 byte), area_number. Encoding: 0-based area index within the world; combined with world byte $075F; loader at CPU $9C13 indexes the header table $9CB4[$075F] + $0760 → $9CBC[], stored into $0750 (AreaType), which sets $074E/$074F and the layout/enemy pointers. Proof: paused at title, wrote $075F=1, $075C=1, $0760=2, pressed Start → loaded WORLD 2-2 underwater (HUD 2-2, blue water/coral). Also $0760=1 alone (world 1) loaded a different area (fortress+pipes). And $075C does not affect the load.\n\n2. Vertical page byte: $00B5 (CPU RAM, 1 byte), high byte of Mario's 16-bit vertical position; low byte $00CE. Total y = $00B5*256 + $00CE. Proof: watched during a jump + pit fall: $00CE wrapped 225→12 etc. while $00B5 stepped 1→2→3→4; death zeroed both; respawn returned to 176/1. Physics routine $BFD7 does the carry add.\n\nLet me write the final answer concisely.\n\nNote about hex formatting: use code spans.\n\nLet me also mention the underwater screenshot proof.\n\nI'll be concise."}
{"type":"text","text":"Both addresses are proven and recorded (promoted into the ROM mapping file).\n\n## 1. Area byte — `$0760` (CPU RAM, 1 byte)\n\n**Name:** `area_number` — which area of the world the level loader loads.\n**Encoding:** a plain 0‑based area index. The loader at CPU `$9C13` does:\n`LDY $075F` (world) → `LDA $9CB4,Y` (per‑world base) → `CLC/ADC $0760` → `TAY` → `LDA $9CBC,Y`; `$9C03` stores that header byte into `$0750` (AreaType), from which `$074E` (palette block), `$074F` (area index) and the layout/enemy pointers (`$9D2C/$9D4E`, `$9CE4/$9D06`) all derive. So the loaded area = header table entry `[$9CB4[$075F] + $0760]`, i.e. `$075F` picks the world and **`$0760` picks the area within it**.\n\n**Proof (write → Start → screenshot):** paused on the title screen (`$0770`=0, `$075F`=`$075C`=`$0760`=0), wrote `$075F`=1, `$075C`=1 and `$0760`=2; the write held for 2 stepped frames, then `play_input [{start,6},{},260]` ran. The game loaded **World 2‑2 under water** — HUD read `WORLD 2-2`, screen showed the blue water backdrop, green sea floor, pink coral and a bubble. Control runs: writing only `$0760`=1 (world 1 unchanged) also loaded a different area (brick fortress with warp pipes). Writing `$075C` alone does **not** change the loaded area (with `$075F`=1, `$075C`=1 but `$0760`=0 the loader used header `$9CBC[5]`=`$28`) — `$075C` is HUD/card only. World companion byte: `$075F`.\n\n## 2. Vertical page byte — `$00B5` (CPU RAM, 1 byte)\n\n**Name:** `player_y_page` — high byte / screen page of Mario's vertical position.\n**Encoding:** Mario's vertical position is 16‑bit: low byte `$00CE`, high byte `$00B5`, total y = `$00B5*256 + $00CE`. The vertical‑move routine at CPU `$BFD7` adds the signed speed `$009F` to the pair with carry:\n`LDA $9F,X` → sign‑extend → `ADC $CE,X / STA $CE,X` → `LDA $B5,X / ADC $07 / STA $B5,X`.\n\n**Proof (watching a pit fall, as the task suggests):** from the World 1‑1 start I ran `play_input [{right,250},{right+a,24},{right,326}]` with a per‑frame watch on `$00CE` and `$00B5`. After the jump, `$00CE` fell into a pit and wrapped `225→12`, `223→10`, `222→4`, while `$00B5` stepped `1` (standing) → `2` → `3` → `4` — exactly one page per 256 px of fall. The fall death then zeroed the pair (both 0) and after respawn both returned to `$00CE`=176, `$00B5`=1. The drawn sprite's screen y is the low byte `$00CE` (copied to `$03B8` by the `$F171` routine), so `$00B5` is the off‑screen vertical page.\n\nBoth were `record_mapping`'d (source `disassembly`, confidence 1) and `promote_mappings` saved them — the file now holds 27 addresses."}
